    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "h230r3e",
      "content": "1 litre = 1000 millilitres"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "7vxbkad",
      "content": "1 tonne = 1000 kilograms"
    },
    {
      "id": "th6x35v",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "DENSITY"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "hdvpdqs",
      "content": "Density is defined as mass per unit volume. Formula:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "7kj2oto",
      "content": "Density = Mass / Volume Unit: kg/m³"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ndj7vij",
      "content": "If density is more, object is heavier. If density is less, object is lighter."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "tuc9904",
      "content": "Example:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "6z2nurq",
      "content": "Iron sinks in water because its density is greater than water."
    },
    {
      "id": "r3g49o2",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "DIMENSIONAL FORMULA"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "q0pq2tz",
      "content": "Dimensional formula represents the relation of a physical quantity with mass (M), length"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ayw02ar",
      "content": "(L) and time (T)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "qc8uc4y",
      "content": "Speed = M⁰L¹T⁻¹ Acceleration = M⁰L¹T⁻² Force = M¹L¹T⁻²"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "hosepwd",
      "content": "Work = M¹L²T⁻² Power = M¹L²T⁻³"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "7rrbbix",
      "content": "Pressure = M¹L⁻¹T⁻²"
    },
    {
      "id": "2ax62v2",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "USES OF DIMENSIONAL FORMULA"
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "Light year, Astronomical Unit (AU), and Parsec are units of DISTANCE, not time.",
        "Quantities like Relative Density, Strain, and Refractive Index are DIMENSIONLESS.",
        "Work, Energy, and Torque share the same dimensional formula: [M¹ L² T⁻²].",
        "Impulse and Linear Momentum have identical dimensions: [M¹ L¹ T⁻¹]."
      ],
      "id": "3tbmwg0"
    },
